Lazy loading defers work until it's needed. It applies at three scales, and all three matter.

```html
<!-- 1. Images. Native, one attribute, no JS. -->
<img src="below-fold.avif" width="800" height="450"
     loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="...">

<!-- NEVER on the LCP image - it delays the fetch until layout runs -->
<img src="hero.avif" width="1200" height="600" fetchpriority="high" alt="...">

<!-- iframes too: third-party embeds are often the heaviest thing on a page -->
<iframe src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/..." loading="lazy"></iframe>
```

```js
// 2. Routes. The single biggest bundle win in most SPAs.
const Settings = lazy(() => import('./routes/Settings'));
const Reports  = lazy(() => import('./routes/Reports'));

// 3. Heavy below-the-fold components, loaded on approach rather than on mount
const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  for (const e of entries) {
    if (!e.isIntersecting) continue;
    import('./charts/HeavyChart').then(m => m.mount(e.target));
    io.unobserve(e.target);
  }
}, { rootMargin: '200px' });   // start 200px early so it is ready on arrival

document.querySelectorAll('[data-chart]').forEach(el => io.observe(el));
```

Route splitting is usually the largest single win: a dashboard with eight routes typically ships one 1.2MB bundle, and splitting it means a first visit downloads 250KB instead. The `rootMargin: '200px'` detail is what makes lazy loading feel invisible — the work starts before the user reaches the element.
